course: ai
lesson: 8
title: Asking well, being polite, knowing when to stop
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A chatbot answers what you ask, so asking well matters: give details, ask for steps, and ask where the facts come from. "Tell me about volcanoes" gets a blur; "Explain in five steps why volcanoes erupt, for a 9-year-old" gets something useful.
explain: Remember what it cannot do. It cannot look out of the window, it cannot keep a secret like a friend, because chats may be stored, and it often cannot truly explain its own mistakes; its explanations of itself may be guesses too.
explain: It can also mix things up: "colour" one day and "color" the next, or two famous people with the same surname blended into one.
explain: Its name is a product name, like a cereal brand, and its "personality" is set by its makers and can change overnight after an update.
explain: Most of all, a bot never gets tired of you, so you are the one who has to choose when to stop.
fact: Clear, detailed questions get clearer answers.
fact: Asking a bot for steps and for its sources makes its answers easier to check.
fact: Bots learned from British and American English and may mix spellings like "colour" and "color".
fact: Bots cannot keep secrets like a friend; conversations may be saved.
fact: A bot's name is a product name chosen by a company.
fact: Bots often cannot look inside themselves, so their explanations of their own mistakes may be made up.
fact: Bots can mix up two people with similar names.
fact: A bot cannot see outside; some assistants can look up the weather online, but your eyes are better for what is in front of you.
fact: You are responsible for the words you use, even if a bot suggested them.
fact: A bot never gets tired, so you have to choose when to stop, like with games and TV.
fact: Companies update their bots, so a bot's tone and "personality" can change.
exercise: Maddie's bot writes "colour" one day and "color" the next. Why? | It learned from British and American English and mixes them | It forgot how to spell overnight and had to guess the next day | It is testing whether Maddie notices | One of the spellings is always a mistake
exercise: The bot gives Jake a funny nickname for his friend that is actually a bit mean. Who is responsible if he uses it? | Jake, because he chooses the words he uses | The bot, because it was the one that made it up | His friend, for being easy to tease | Nobody, it was only a joke
exercise: Mia asks the bot to keep a secret. What does an honest bot say? | It can't promise, because chats may be stored | Yes, it will never tell anyone ever, cross its heart | It will keep it if she says please | It will only tell its best friend
exercise: Sam asks the bot its name. What kind of name does it have? | A product name, like a brand | A first name given to it by its mother | A secret name only it knows about | A name it chose all by itself
exercise: The bot explains why it got the strawberry question wrong. How far should Lily trust that? | Carefully, its explanation of itself may be a guess | Completely, because bots always know exactly why they fail | Not at all, bots never explain anything | Only if the explanation is long
exercise: Connor's dad shows him how to ask better. Which question is best? | "Explain in steps, for a 9-year-old, and say where the facts come from" | "Tell me stuff" | "Volcanoes?" | "Say everything you know right now"
exercise: The bot mixes up two famous people with the same surname. Where should Chloe check? | In an encyclopedia | With the same bot again | With a bot in another language | Nowhere, it must be right
exercise: Maddie asks if it is raining. What is the easiest way to find out? | Look out of the window | Ask the bot three more times | Ask the bot to make a guess | Check yesterday's weather
exercise: Jake's mum sets 20 minutes of chatbot play, then outside. Why is a limit sensible? | A bot never gets tired, so you must choose when to stop | Bots break after 20 minutes and need to be fixed by the company | Fresh air makes the bot faster | The bot needs time to sleep
exercise: Mia's bot is robotic one day and cheerful the next. Why? | Its makers updated it, and its tone can change | It woke up in a bad mood that day and felt grumpy | It is angry with Mia | It has a twin that answers some days
exercise: What makes answers easier to check? | Asking the bot for steps and sources | Asking very short questions | Asking in capital letters | Asking late at night when it's quiet
exercise: Before using a bot's words about a person, what should you ask yourself? | "Would this hurt?" | "Is this the longest answer?" | "Did the answer rhyme?" | "Was the answer fast?"
exercise: Why can't the bot tell Maddie about the rain from what it sees? | It has no eyes or window | It is not allowed to talk about weather | Rain is a secret | It only knows about snow
exercise: What should you do with real secrets? | Not tell them to bots | Tell them to bots, they forget | Post them online for friends | Type them into the bot very fast
test: "i asked it to pinky promise to keep my secret and it did, so it's safe" Is it? | No, chats may be stored, so don't tell bots secrets | Yes, a pinky promise is binding for bots, just like for people | Yes, bots have no memory at all | No, but only on weekends
test: What would happen if you asked "tell me about dogs" instead of "list five things a puppy needs"? | You would probably get a vaguer answer | You would get exactly the same answer | The bot would refuse the short one | The bot would ask your dog
test: Your mate calls the bot "Gerald" and insists that's its real name. What is true? | Its only name is a product name; Gerald is a nickname | Gerald is its secret birth name that it told him | All bots are secretly called Gerald | Bots change their names every day
test: A bot says "I got that wrong because I was tired." What should you think? | Bots don't get tired; its explanation may be made up | It needs a rest, so try again tomorrow when it's had a sleep | Bots get tired after lunch | It is telling the exact truth
test: "bot said that nickname, not me, so i'm not mean" Is that right? | You chose to use it, so it's on you | Yes, the bot is the one who is being mean | Yes, words from bots don't count at all | Nobody can ever be blamed for nicknames
test: A cartoon shows a chatbot peering out of a window to check the rain. What's wrong? | Chatbots have no eyes; some only look up weather online | Chatbots only look out of doors, never out of windows | Nothing, that's how they check | Chatbots can see only clouds
test: Mia's bot suddenly sounds different after the weekend. Most likely reason? | The company updated it | It went on holiday | It has caught a cold | It is a different bot every Monday
test: It's 9 pm and Sam has chatted with the bot for two hours. The bot still says "Anything else?" What should he notice? | The bot never tires, so he must choose to stop | The bot wants him to stay up all night chatting with it | The bot will be upset if he leaves | He should chat until the bot says stop
test: The bot writes a fact about a famous scientist, but mixes in a footballer with the same surname. What happened? | Similar names blended in its patterns | The scientist also played football for a famous team | The bot is joking on purpose | The footballer invented science
test: Your school uses British spelling, but the bot writes "color". What do you write? | "colour", the spelling your school uses | "color", because the bot said so | Both spellings in the same word | Whatever is shortest
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the capacity of a chatbot to preserve confidences? | It cannot guarantee confidentiality, conversations being liable to storage | It is bound to absolute secrecy in respect of all users and all conversations | It forgets every disclosure immediately and irrevocably | It discloses confidences only to other chatbots
exam: Identify the formulation of a request most likely to elicit a useful and verifiable answer. | A detailed request specifying steps, audience and sources | A request consisting of a single word | A request expressed entirely in capital letters | A request repeated several times in quick succession
exam: It is given that a chatbot offers an explanation of its own previous error. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The explanation may itself be conjectural, the chatbot being unable to inspect its own workings | The explanation is invariably accurate and complete | The explanation is furnished by its engineers in real time | The explanation proves that the error was the user's
exam: Ascertain the party upon whom the duty of terminating a conversation with a chatbot principally falls. | The user, the chatbot never becoming fatigued | The chatbot, upon the exhaustion of its energy | The company, at the close of each business day | No party, conversations ending of their own accord
